$DIR/main.p:14:10: p is a view of Node that may change while another task uses it, so it cannot be loaded once tasks may run
    Loading p here gives this task p, a *Node that other tasks may reach too:
    every task that loads it can reach the same Node. A Node is freed, dropped,
    moved or overwritten at main.p:21 (in main) once tasks may run, so the
    compiler cannot tell that this one is not.
    A view may cross to another task only if what it points to cannot change
    while both may use it, or every change is synchronized: a package variable
    that is not written once tasks run, a struct whose fields are never changed
    once it is shared or are used only under a lock of the same struct or
    atomically, memory of a pluto/sync type, or the object of a synced[T].
    Go leaves data races to a run-time detector that a program must be run
    under, and a racing Go program can see values half written. Plutonium tasks
    run in parallel with no such detector, so safe code must be free of data
    races, and the compiler checks it from the program itself, with no
    annotations.
    Pass a copy of the value instead of a pointer to it, or move an owner (such
    as a vec.Vec) to the task so that only it has the memory.
    To share changing data between tasks, put it in a synced[T] and hold a
    sync.Mutex in it at every access, or use a package variable guarded by a
    lock.
    (see "Data race freedom" in the Plutonium spec, section 19.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3501" for a longer explanation)
